# 12. FIRESTORE ARCHITECTURE

Inspect the current database before making schema changes.

Extend existing collections where appropriate.

Potential collections:

### `/userPreferences/{userId}`

Stores:
- Theme.
- Accent colour.
- Feed density.
- Reduced-motion preference.
- Default feed.
- Anime interests.
- Genre preferences.
- Recommendation preferences.
- Content preferences.
- Dashboard layout.

### `/userAnimeLists/{userId}/items/{animeId}`

Stores:
- Anime catalogue ID.
- Title snapshot.
- Cover image reference.
- Status.
- Episodes watched.
- Total episodes when verified.
- Rating.
- Start date.
- Completion date.
- Personal notes.
- Created timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

### `/userCollections/{collectionId}`

Stores:
- Owner ID.
- Title.
- Description.
- Cover image reference.
- Visibility.
- Created timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

### `/userCollections/{collectionId}/items/{itemId}`

Stores:
- Anime catalogue ID.
- Title snapshot.
- Cover image reference.
- Position/order.
- Created timestamp.

### Recommendation feedback

Use a suitable structure such as:

`/recommendationFeedback/{userId}/items/{animeId}`

Store:
- Feedback type.
- Optional reason.
- Created timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

### Dashboard configuration

This may be stored inside `/userPreferences/{userId}` or another suitable user-owned document.

### Architecture requirements

- Follow existing naming conventions where possible.
- Avoid duplicating existing user profile data.
- Use server timestamps.
- Use transactions or batched writes for related operations.
- Avoid unbounded arrays in Firestore documents.
- Use pagination for large collections.
- Create composite indexes where required.
- Document all new collections and fields.

Do not deploy destructive schema migrations.

